From c6a21e20ee3d33246ceb6180cb4d19f2f9aed604 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 27 Jul 2026 12:22:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Use Thmanyah Sans as the single font for Arabic and English

The site was pinned to IBM Plex Sans Arabic and Thmanyah Sans could not be
applied. Two things were blocking it:

- globals.css forced `font-family: 'IBM Plex Sans Arabic' ... !important` on a
  universal selector, overriding every other font declaration on the page.
- LanguageContext assigned to `document.documentElement.className` on mount,
  which wiped the classes the root layout sets on <html>. That destroyed the
  next/font variable class (along with `h-full` and `antialiased`), so the font
  was lost at hydration no matter how layout.js was configured.

Changes:

- Load Thmanyah Sans via next/font/local from WOFF2 instead of OTF (~3x
  smaller) and drop IBM Plex, including the render-blocking Google Fonts
  <link> and its preconnects.
- Add a --font-sans token that resolves to the next/font family and falls back
  to the plain @font-face declaration, then to system UI fonts.
- Point the universal rule at --font-sans and replace the hardcoded IBM Plex
  strings in inline styles.
- Toggle the theme class instead of overwriting className.

Thmanyah Sans covers Latin, Arabic and Arabic-Indic digits, so one family
serves both languages. Verified in Chromium: every element on /, /spaces,
/book-tour, /checkout, /erp, /erp/crm, /member, /member/billing and /auth
resolves to Thmanyah Sans in both locales and in light and dark themes, with
no duplicate font downloads.
